WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:04.379
[Narrator:] Smart girl, Mary Sue. She's learned early to brush her teeth

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.980
the right way at the right time.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:06.890
[Mary Sue stands in front of the mirror brushing her teeth.]

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:08.990
[Narrator:] Brush your teeth immediately after
eating.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:11.070
If brushing is impossible, rinse the mouth
with water.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:11.879
[Mary Sue finds a water fountain to rinse
her mouth out.]

00:00:11.879 --> 00:00:14.389
[Narrator:] Brush the upper teeth downward,
the lower teeth upward.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.490
For healthier teeth, a prettier smile, learn
to brush your teeth correctly.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:22.330
[Mary Sue brushes her teeth happily in the
mirror and smiles.]

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.100
[Narrator:] Good food for good health. But
for good dental health,

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.880
what not to eat is also important.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:31.960
[An adolescent boy sits at a dinner table
passing around a plate of chicken.]

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.500
[Narrator:] Dental scientists report that
sugar is the major cause of tooth decay.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:35.780
[A bowl of caramel candy quickly evaporates.]

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.800
[Narrator:] Avoid candy and other sweet foods between meals.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.000
Substitute fruit, nuts, or popcorn.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.359
[The boy switches from his candy to take a
bite out of an apple instead.]

00:00:40.359 --> 00:00:42.940
[Narrator:] Cut down on sweets. Cut down on
tooth decay.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.720
[The boy smiles into the camera to show his healthy
set of teeth.]

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.600
[Narrator:] Johnny has a new tooth, and an
important one it is.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.399
[Johnny looks in the mirror with his fingers
in his mouth trying to feel his new tooth.]

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:57.690
[Narrator:] Children's first, or baby teeth
affect general health. They're necessary as

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.170
a guide for permanent teeth to follow.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:00:59.839
[Johnny receives a glass of milk to drink
from his mother.]

00:00:59.839 --> 00:01:03.190
[Narrator:] For clear speech, for giving form
and symmetry to the face.

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:04.409
Protect your children's first teeth.

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:05.150
[Johnny plays with the mirror in the dentist's
hand to look at the inside of his mouth.]

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.970
[Narrator:] Help your youngster toward lifelong
dental health.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.320
[Narrator:] Smart girl, Mary Sue. She's learned to brush her teeth the right way at the right time.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.380
[Mary Sue stands in front of the mirror brushing her teeth.]

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:23.210
[Narrator:] Brush your teeth immediately after
eating or rinse the mouth with water.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.720
[Mary Sue then finds a water fountain to rinse
her mouth out.]

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:28.310
[Narrator:] Brush the upper teeth downward, the lower teeth upward.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.380
This is National Children's Dental Health week.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.690
Your dentist says "Learn to brush your teeth correctly."

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:34.000
[Mary Sue brushes her teeth happily in the
mirror and smiles.]

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.060
[Narrator:] Good food for good health. But
for good dental health,

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.900
what not to eat is also important.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:44.570
[An adolescent boy sits at a dinner table
passing around a plate of chicken.]

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.290
[Narrator:] Dental scientists report that
sugar is the main cause of tooth decay.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:48.050
[A bowl of caramel candy quickly evaporates.]

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:51.500
[Narrator:] This is National Children's Dental
Health week. You can substitute fruit for

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.180
sweet foods between meals.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.960
Cut down on sweets. Cut down on tooth decay.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.060
[Smiling adolescent boy.]

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:05.400
[Narrator:] Johnny has a new tooth, and an
important one it is.

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:06.320
[Johnny looks in the mirror with his fingers
in his mouth trying to feel his new tooth.]

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:08.800
[Narrator:] This is National Children's Dental Health Week and your dentist

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.420
wants you to know that children's first teeth are important.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.380
[Johnny receives a glass of milk to drink
from his mother.]

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.950
[Narrator:] They're necessary as guides for
the permanent which are to follow.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:18.250
[Narrator:] Protect your children's first
teeth. Help your youngsters

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:19.960
toward lifelong dental health.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.800
[Johnny plays with the mirror in the dentist's
hand to look at the inside of his mouth.]

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.760
[Narrator:] For sparkling teeth and a lifetime
of dental health, start dental care early.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.220
[A woman smiles into the mirror as she watches
her daughter carefully brush her teeth.]

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:35.360
[Narrator:] Children who begin proper dental
care at an early age form good dental habits

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.230
that last a lifetime.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:42.970
Let your child enjoy lifetime dental health
by starting proper dental care early.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.060
This message from the Cleveland Health Council.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:56.180
[Narrator:] Your Dental Society Presents...
a Good News Story about Dentures.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:58.820
[Narrator:] A public service message about
dental health.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:02:59.880
[A dentist unrolls a poster that reads "Dental Health"]

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.480
[Narrator:] First of all, follow good dental
practices so you can keep your natural teeth

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.640
as long as possible.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:05.620
[An image of a toothbrush multiplies into
another toothbrush]

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.900
[Narrator:] On the other hand, if you are
unfortunate enough

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.950
to have lost your natural teeth, you can obtain substitutes.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.060
[Three men and a woman appear smiling.]

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.940
[Narrator:] One out of four Americans is already
wearing dentures, but you'd never know it.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:21.130
Modern dentures are so natural-looking. Lack
of teeth ages you, hinders your digestion,

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:23.420
handicaps you in many ways.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.380
Careful study by your dentist is the first
step towards good-looking dentures

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.520
that are functional and comfortable, too.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.380
[Narrator:] Your dentures will be designed
to meet your personal needs.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.660
With them you'll be healthier, happier, better-looking.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:40.130
You'll be your same not-so-old self once again.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:41.440
[A man smiles to reveal his sparkly new dentures.]

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.940
[Narrator:] For a better life in every way,
your Dental Society urges you

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.800
to have your teeth checked regularly.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:59.770
[Narrator:] Your Dental Society in cooperation
with the American Dental Association presents

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.870
"A Toothsome Tale"

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:05.810
The story of how man has brushed his teeth
down through the ages.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:06.739
[A caveman picks his teeth with a bone.]

00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:11.839
[Narrator:] Long ago, toothbrushes were rather
basic. Then, man began to depend on

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:16.600
Mother Nature. Now, we use small, straight toothbrushes.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:20.720
Two of them so one of them so one will always
be dry and fresh.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:27.620
Brushing top teeth down, bottom teeth up,
and carefully cleaning the insides and chewing surfaces.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:28.640
[A toothbrush is shown brushing a set of teeth up, down, and then inside.]

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.320
[Narrator:] Most important of all, today,
smart people brush their teeth immediately

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.020
after every meal. And when that's not convenient,
they rinse their mouths with water.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:37.880
[A man sips a cup of water in order to rinse
out his mouth after eating a meal.]

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:42.320
[Narrator:] So for better dental health, brush
properly and brush immediately after meals

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:43.699
with a good toothbrush.

00:04:43.699 --> 00:04:44.860
[A dentist holds up a chart that list ways
to maintain proper dental health.]

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:49.060
[Narrator:] This public service message has
been presented by your Dental Society in cooperation

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:51.880
with the American Dental Association.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.800
[A group of free-floating animated heads are talking to each other, mouths opening and closing.]

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:08.260
[Narrator:] These days everybody's talking
about, reading about, and hearing about diets.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.070
[A man smokes his pipe in a chair watching
television.]

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:13.530
[Narrator:] Children, too, need a well-balanced
diet to promote good general health

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:15.190
as well as to develop healthy teeth and gums.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:16.900
[A dentist inspects a little boy's teeth.]

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:21.240
[Narrator:] Actually, it's a simple diet.
Every day your child needs three glasses of milk.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.199
Two or more servings of green or yellow vegetables.

00:05:24.199 --> 00:05:31.199
Two fresh fruits, one raw, including citrus
fruits. An egg and meat, fish,

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.040
poultry, or cheese. Whole grain cereal and buttered bread.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.280
These are the foods your children need to
develop strong healthy teeth.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.760
[A tooth is shown with a cape on and an image of children drinking a glass of milk using straws appears.]

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:45.639
[Narrator:] They're good for you too. To help
prevent tooth decay, substitute fruits, nuts,

00:05:45.639 --> 00:05:47.610
and popcorn for sweets between meals.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:48.660
[Fruits and nuts are shown beside a large
bowl of popcorn.]

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:53.240
[Narrator:] This has been a public service
message presented to you by your Dental Society

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:57.680
and the American Dental Association.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.900
[Narrator:] Introducing Liz the Quiz Whiz.
She knows all the answers in the category

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:14.490
of the importance of first teeth, so-called
baby teeth. Do you?

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:17.460
[Liz takes a bow and trumpets begin to sound.]

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.490
[Narrator:] How many teeth in the first set,
Liz?

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:20.180
[Liz:] Twenty.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:22.710
[Narrator:] Why are these first teeth so important?

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:26.860
[Liz:] Scientifically speaking, the deciduous,
medial, and lateral incisors form...

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:32.320
[Narrator:] Yes, Liz. First teeth are important because they maintain space for your permanent teeth.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:34.780
When do permanent or second teeth appear?

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:36.270
[Liz:] The time of incisory...

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.730
[Narrator:] Yes, Liz. The first permanent teeth, usually six-year molars,

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:41.920
appear at about six years of age.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:46.389
Their loss handicaps a child permanently.
Children should have their first dental check-up

00:06:46.389 --> 00:06:50.180
when they're two years old or when they have
all their first teeth.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:54.759
So they'll win one of the most important prizes of all: sound, healthy, permanent teeth.

00:06:54.759 --> 00:06:55.389
[A boy and girl smile at each other showing
their healthy teeth.]

00:06:55.389 --> 00:06:58.630
[Narrator:] This public service message has
been brought to you by your Dental Society

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:00.949
in cooperation with the American Dental Association.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:10.820
[An older man is shown with his dog. His mouth
appears pinched.]

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:15.040
[Narrator:] Poor Sam. He's worried. Sam is
a social outcast.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:20.060
At work, he's no sensation either. Even though he's only thirty-five.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:24.639
He loves good food but can't eat properly.
What's Sam's trouble?

00:07:24.639 --> 00:07:28.539
Dental disease has robbed him of his teeth.
Those he has are okay,

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:30.140
but he just doesn't have enough of them.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:31.720
[Sam opens his mouth to show how many teeth
he is missing.]

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:36.840
[Narrator:] With only six teeth, Sam can't
chew food well. Result, poor digestion, poor nutrition.

00:07:37.509 --> 00:07:42.589
Sam goes to his dentist for professional advice.
His dentist tells him he needs partial dentures.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:47.680
[Narrator:] Now look at Sam. Handsome, personable,
self-confident Sam.

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:49.429
[Smiling people stand on either side of Sam
and pin a first place medal on him.]

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:51.920
[Narrator:] No digestion or nutrition troubles
now.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.700
Sam's partial dentures are comfortable, attractive, serviceable, easy to get used to.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:08:01.870
Fact is, one out of four Americans wears dentures today.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:07.680
This message has been presented by your dental society and the American Dental Association.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:18.640
[Narrator:] Smile pretty, everyone. Oh, poor
Peggy. Too bad parents neglect baby teeth.

00:08:18.759 --> 00:08:19.940
Youngsters are so sensitive about appearance.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:20.920
[Peggy cries after being ashamed to smile
in her class picture because of her teeth.]

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.440
[Narrator:] And, neglected baby teeth can cause permanent teeth to come in damaged and irregular.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.700
Give your child the gift of a happy smile.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:29.100
Your child's first teeth are important.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:31.740
[Peggy smiles to show her new healthy teeth at the dentist office while her mom and the dentist watch.]

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:39.260
[Little boy eats a lollipop while dressed
as a cowboy.]

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:42.380
[Narrator:] Hold it partner. Old villain tooth
decay is hiding out in your mouth.

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:45.970
Ready to attack your teeth, cause cavities
every time you eat.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:47.630
[The villainous tooth decay is shown inside
the boy's mouth shooting his teeth with cavity spray.]

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:51.500
[Narrator:] Here's your troubleshooting toothbrush.
It sweeps away food particles.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.020
Helps knock out tooth decay.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:54.360
[Toothbrush rushes in and wipes away tooth
decay. The boy smiles.]

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.880
Remember partner, reach for your toothbrush
after every meal.

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:08.560
[Narrator:] Meet Susie Brown, who wins the
prize for her sparkling smile.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:10.360
But wait, a trophy also goes to Susie's mother.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:12.140
[Susie gets a trophy from a man standing on a stepladder. The same man hands her mom a trophy.]

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:15.560
[Narrator:] Mother started taking Susie to
the dentist when she was only two.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:18.459
He made sure Susie's permanant teeth would grow in sound and straight.

00:09:18.459 --> 00:09:19.679
[Susie's mother takes her to the dentist when she was younger and Susie smiles to show her healthy teeth.]

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.820
[Narrator:] Be like Susie's mother. Help your child to healthy teeth.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:34.760
[Narrator:] Funny how we sweat and slave over things which, um, soon go out of style.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:35.700
[A man is washing his car when another car rides by and splashes water on him.]

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:40.640
[Narrator:] Yet we neglect our teeth. Which,
with care and attention can last a lifetime.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:43.860
Brush your teeth immediately after eating.
Give them the regular care they need.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:44.840
[The man brushes his teeth and smiles.]

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:47.310
[Narrator:] Smile your way through life. Your
teeth can last a lifetime.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:48.810
[A wife and children appear next to the man.]

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:01.460
[Narrator:] Teenage Tommy wins all gals with a secret weapon. His pearly teeth and gleaming grin.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:02.940
[A girl bypasses another boy to walk side-by-side
with Tommy.]

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:06.630
[Narrator:] The smart set knows too many sweets are for squares. They cause cavities,

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:10.190
make you feel funny about your looks. So snack on fresh fruit, milk, or nuts.

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:11.460
[The boy who was left alone eats some candy while Tommy offers the girl an apple then kisses her on the cheek.]

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:15.120
[Narrator:] Teens with healthy teeth really
rate.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:34.920
[Music]

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:39.700
[Narrator:] This is Billy. Billy is a little
boy who is always happy and healthy

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:43.540
and who always wears a big smile.

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:47.450
Everyone likes to see Billy smile, for he
has clean, white teeth.

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:49.769
[His family happily watches as Billy joyfully
smiles.]

00:10:49.769 --> 00:10:55.450
[Narrator:] But one day not long ago, Billy
noticed that one of his teeth was loose.

00:10:55.450 --> 00:10:57.550
Soon it was loose enough to pull out without hurting.

00:10:57.550 --> 00:10:58.600
[Billy pulls his tooth out]

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:03.170
[Narrator:] Tonight you should place the tooth
under your pillow, said his mother.

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:06.230
And in the morning you might find a surprise
under your pillow.

00:11:06.230 --> 00:11:07.920
[Billy places the tooth under his pillow.]

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:12.400
[Narrator:] So that night Billy placed the
tooth under his pillow.

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:15.720
Soon after falling asleep, he began to dream.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:21.850
And in his dream he saw his tooth. It was
larger than a tooth really is,

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:25.339
and it could move and talk.

00:11:25.339 --> 00:11:32.339
Hello, it said. I'm Tommy Tooth. I just wanted
to say goodbye.

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:38.260
Why must you go away, Billy asked Tommy Tooth,
setting him on his knee.

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:43.690
Because you are growing up, and you need a
big grown-up tooth, Tommy Tooth answered.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:48.240
Since you were a very little boy, I have helped
you chew your food.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:54.470
I have stood straight in line, so you could
speak clearly and have a nice smile.

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:58.670
But now, I am no longer big enough to do the job well.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:01.800
So a bigger tooth will soon fill the space I am leaving.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:03.080
[Tommy Tooth lies down]

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:10.160
[Narrator:] And...*yawns*... I should take
a nice long rest somewhere.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:13.969
But before I go, let me warn you, said Tommy Tooth.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:22.120
Your new tooth must last you the rest of your life, so take good care of it.

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:33.220
For good health you should have meat, vegetables and fruits, milk, and bread or cereal every day.

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:39.910
Be careful about what you eat and when you eat it.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:44.400
For instance, sweets eaten between meals can spoil your appetite.

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:46.540
[Billy rejects his dinner because he has eaten
sweets between meals.]

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:50.520
[Narrator:] Also, because they are sticky,
sweets cling to your teeth.

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:56.660
And sweet foods that stick to the teeth can
cause your teeth a lot of damage

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:59.839
if not cleaned off immediately.

00:12:59.839 --> 00:13:06.269
That is why you should clean your teeth right
after eating, if possible.

00:13:06.269 --> 00:13:11.720
If you do not have your toothbrush with you
when you're away from home,

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:16.260
eating an apple will help clean your teeth,
and apples taste good, too.

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:18.260
[Billy eats an apple]

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:23.580
[Narrator:] Besides, apples and other fruits
give you vitamins and minerals and lots of energy.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:29.870
Finally, you should have a dentist look at
your teeth regularly.

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:37.350
If you do all of these things carefully, especially
while you are growing up

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:43.040
you will keep your second teeth a long, long time.

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:46.140
Even as long as you live.

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:50.580
But now, you must go back to sleep, for rest is also important.

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:54.430
Goodbye, said Billy.

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:58.240
And thank you. I'll remember what you told
me.

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:02.670
And with that Billy's dream ended and he went
on sleeping peacefully.

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:05.840
[Birds chirp outside]

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:08.900
[Billy wakes up and checks under his pillow,
finding money in place of the tooth.]

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:11.500
[Narrator:] The next morning, instead of the
tooth under his pillow,

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:14.200
he found a bright shiny coin.

00:14:14.209 --> 00:14:18.180
Billy showed his mother the coin and told
her about the dream.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:21.920
The dentist has told you the same things before,
his mother said.

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:27.660
But now that Tommy Tooth told you in your
dream, you will remember better than ever.

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:33.880
And do you know, Billy did remember.

00:14:54.420 --> 00:15:00.660
[Animated bulldozers are heading to a construction
site.]

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:11.980
[Narrator:] Few sights so stir the heart of
man. Few opportunities so fire his imagination

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:17.260
as...yes...the chance to be a sidewalk superintendent!

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:19.300
[Men rush to line up a sign that reads For
Sidewalk Superintendents]

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:23.899
[Narrator:] For example, here comes Henry
J. Whortle, on his way to an important appointment.

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:28.280
[Henry:] Heya, Hey, Hey, Hey what's going
on? Hey, let me through.

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:30.100
[Henry pushes his way through the group  men where he can see through the hole in the gate.]

00:15:30.260 --> 00:15:32.640
[Henry:] Ohhhhhhh, they're fixing the bridge.

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:39.840
Say, there that looks like a big
mouth. With a tooth missing. What'd I say?

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:41.820
[Narrator:] What's the matter Henry? Does
that remind you of your own missing...

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:43.380
[Henry opens his mouth to reveal one of his
bottom teeth is missing.]

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:47.240
[Narrator:] But what's a missing tooth? You
have more important things to think about.

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:54.240
[Henry:] Oh sure. Sure I have. And say, why
all the fuss? There's only one measly little

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:56.480
stone missing out of that great big bridge.

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:00.020
[Narrator:] Glad you brought that up, Henry.

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:02.820
Gives us a chance to show off our newest piece of equipment.

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:04.040
The future-scope.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:05.420
[A giant machine appears next to Henry]

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:06.780
[Henry:] Future-scope? Eh, what for?

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:09.980
[Narrator:] To foretell the future, naturally.

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:15.340
Here, see what the future of this bridge would be if it weren't being fixed right now.

00:16:15.779 --> 00:16:17.149
[Henry:] Uh, what do I do?

00:16:17.149 --> 00:16:22.829
[Narrator:] Just dial to Bridge and press
the button. It's a big bridge but like you

00:16:22.829 --> 00:16:25.079
said, just one measly little stone is missing.

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:30.040
[Henry:] Heya, Hey, Hey look the other stones
are moving.

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:33.760
The whole bridge is getting out of shape. Ohhhhhhh.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:35.820
[A stone that came out of the bridge falls
out on the future scope and hits Henry's foot.]

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:42.000
[Henry:] Ouch. Hmm, just one stone starting
all that.

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:45.140
[Narrator:] That's what could happen if they
didn't do something about it.

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:50.019
[Henry:] Uhh hey. This future-scope work on
people too?

00:16:50.019 --> 00:16:51.389
[Narrator:] Yes.

00:16:51.389 --> 00:16:59.129
[Henry:] Me, maybe? Could it show me five,
ten years from now? Like Charles Boyer maybe.

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:01.550
[A man in a Middle Eastern style hat speaks
to a woman passing him.]

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:03.350
[Stranger in hat:] Darling, come with me to
the casbah.

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:05.780
[Narrator:] Go on, try it.

00:17:05.949 --> 00:17:11.129
[Henry:] Hahaha, I can hardly wait. Say, uh,
Whortle, Henry J.

00:17:11.129 --> 00:17:13.309
[The name Whortle, Henry J. appears on the
future scope.]

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:17.739
[Henry:] Ehhh wait why do I.. this can't be.
There must be some mistake.

00:17:17.739 --> 00:17:21.859
[Future Henry:] The future-scope never makes
mistakes.

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:27.420
[Henry:] A handsome guy like me. How, how,
how could it happen?

00:17:27.429 --> 00:17:28.929
[Henry thinks about the bridge falling apart.]

00:17:28.929 --> 00:17:33.869
[Henry:] That can happen to me? Urghh because
I lost one measly tooth?

00:17:34.229 --> 00:17:39.169
[Narrator:] Henry. Your teeth are like a stone
bridge in many ways.

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:41.840
Durable. Meant to last a lifetime.

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:46.970
But like the stones in the bridge, your teeth
depend upon each other for support.

00:17:46.970 --> 00:17:51.570
[Henry:] Lose one tooth and I can be in for
trouble?

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:56.559
[Narrator:] Yes. Starting with children, it's
wrong to ignore a missing tooth.

00:17:56.559 --> 00:17:58.979
When youngsters lose their first teeth too
early,

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:02.680
a space maintainer can prevent their teeth from shifting.

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:07.120
If this is not done, the permanent teeth can
come in crooked or out of place.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:09.760
It can spoil a child's smile for life.

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:11.160
[A little girl loses a tooth and grows up
with a ruined smile.]

00:18:11.169 --> 00:18:12.340
[Henry:] So what about me?

00:18:12.340 --> 00:18:17.450
[Narrator:] We'll show you. Grown-ups should
have the missing tooth replaced, too,

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:21.270
with a bridge. If it is not replaced...well, just look.

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:22.609
[An x-ray of a mouth is shown with other teeth
drifting into the space where a tooth is missing.]

00:18:22.779 --> 00:18:26.219
[Narrator:] The other teeth may start drifting into the empty space.

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:32.080
These irregularly spaced teeth are harder to clean. So it is easier to get cavities.

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:38.880
And because the teeth don't come together
properly, there's liable to be gum and bone injury.

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:42.940
If you neglect it, there may be infection
of the tissue around the teeth.

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:44.400
[X-ray of teeth shows gum and bone injury
as well as possible places of infection.]

00:18:44.409 --> 00:18:47.909
[Narrator:] And eventually this could lead
to the loss of all your teeth.

00:18:47.909 --> 00:18:49.309
[Henry puts his hand over his mouth]

00:18:49.309 --> 00:18:55.089
[Henry:] All my teeth!? I never realized.
Just letting one tooth go.

00:18:55.460 --> 00:19:01.880
Why didn't somebody tell me? Somebody did. I just remembered.

00:19:01.979 --> 00:19:08.299
My dentist. That's what my important appointment
was. I'm late. Gangwayyyyy!

00:19:08.299 --> 00:19:11.979
[Narrator:] [laughter] Henry's lucky. He realized in time

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:15.460
the damage just one missing tooth can do. How about you?

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:17.360
[Henry runs past the bridge and goes straight to the dentist.]

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:35.780
[Music]

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:38.340
[Screen goes dark.]

